Similarly, plugging results from \eref{r01} and \eref{r02} in \eref{relu3} yields
\begin{equation} 
s_{m+1}^{2}  \approx 0.34(s_{m}^{2} + \mu_{m}^{2}). \enum{relu5}
\end{equation}
Simple manipulations of equations \erefs{r01}{relu5} gives
\begin{equation} 
\mu_{m}^{2} \approx 0.16 \times (0.51)^{m-1} \enum{relu603}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} 
s_{m}^{2} \approx 0.34 \times (0.51)^{m-1}. \enum{relu602}
\end{equation}
for all $m \geq 1$. These approximations are remarkably accurate, as is demonstrated by comparisons with the simulation experiments described in Figure \ref{fig:cs231ncomp}.

Sigmoid activation
For the sigmoid activation defined as
\begin{equation} 
g(x) = \frac{1}{1 + \mbox{e}^{-x}}
\end{equation}
we have $g(0) = 0.5$, $g'(0) = 1/4$. Plugging these values in \eref{in208} yields
\begin{equation} 
v \approx \frac{3.6}{\sqrt{N}}, \enum{relur}
\end{equation} 
To compare the initialization described in \eref{relur} with
the {\it{Xavier initialization}}, I use a simple 10 layer neural network whose architecture is described in Figure \ref{fig:nnarch}. For my experiments, I use the  CIFAR 10 dataset \cite{krizhevsky2009learning} comprising 60,000,  $32 \times 32$ color images evenly split over 10 classes. The dataset comprises 50,000 training examples (which forms the training dataset in my analyses) and 10,000 test examples (which forms the validation dataset in my analyses). 







































